1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,380
In the Arizona desert,

2
00:00:01,860 --> 00:00:03,900
the United States Army once tested a

3
00:00:03,900 --> 00:00:06,460
vehicle that looked less like a truck,

4
00:00:06,900 --> 00:00:09,440
and more like an entire transportation

5
00:00:09,440 --> 00:00:11,700
system moving under its own

6
00:00:11,700 --> 00:00:12,320
power.

7
00:00:12,940 --> 00:00:15,460
It was 572 feet long,

8
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:18,480
13 units in all, a towering

9
00:00:18,480 --> 00:00:20,740
control car followed by cargo

10
00:00:20,740 --> 00:00:22,260
and power sections.

11
00:00:23,060 --> 00:00:25,360
54 wheels could drive the machine

12
00:00:25,360 --> 00:00:25,980
forward.

13
00:00:26,460 --> 00:00:28,580
It carried a crew of six, and

14
00:00:28,580 --> 00:00:31,200
could haul roughly 150 tons

15
00:00:31,200 --> 00:00:32,120
of cargo.

16
00:00:33,040 --> 00:00:35,800
And despite being called an overland train,

17
00:00:36,040 --> 00:00:37,420
it needed no railroad,

18
00:00:38,080 --> 00:00:38,900
no rails,

19
00:00:39,420 --> 00:00:40,620
no prepared highway.

20
00:00:41,300 --> 00:00:43,820
It was designed to keep moving when

21
00:00:43,820 --> 00:00:46,040
conventional transportation infrastructure

22
00:00:46,040 --> 00:00:47,420
simply ended.

23
00:00:48,060 --> 00:00:50,300
This was the LeTourneau TC

24
00:00:50,300 --> 00:00:52,780
497 Overland Train

25
00:00:52,780 --> 00:00:54,000
Mark II.

26
00:00:54,580 --> 00:00:56,980
At the time, the Army described it

27
00:00:56,980 --> 00:00:59,340
as the longest rubber -tired vehicle

28
00:00:59,340 --> 00:01:00,500
in the world.

29
00:01:01,080 --> 00:01:02,920
But its size is not the strangest

30
00:01:02,920 --> 00:01:03,960
part of the story.

31
00:01:04,620 --> 00:01:06,480
Because after the Army took this machine

32
00:01:06,480 --> 00:01:08,820
into the desert and tested it, the

33
00:01:08,820 --> 00:01:11,000
TC 497 did something

34
00:01:11,240 --> 00:01:13,680
that makes its disappearance much more

35
00:01:13,680 --> 00:01:14,380
interesting.

36
00:01:14,980 --> 00:01:17,260
It demonstrated that the idea could

37
00:01:17,260 --> 00:01:18,480
actually work,

38
00:01:18,920 --> 00:01:20,960
and America scrapped it anyway.

39
00:01:21,600 --> 00:01:23,720
The TC 497

40
00:01:23,720 --> 00:01:26,260
did not begin with somebody asking how

41
00:01:26,260 --> 00:01:28,700
large a vehicle could possibly be built.

42
00:01:29,420 --> 00:01:31,580
It began with a much more practical

43
00:01:31,580 --> 00:01:32,300
question.

44
00:01:33,300 --> 00:01:35,960
What happens when the road ends?

45
00:01:36,800 --> 00:01:39,160
Railroads can move enormous quantities of

46
00:01:39,160 --> 00:01:39,660
cargo,

47
00:01:40,200 --> 00:01:42,300
but only where somebody has already laid

48
00:01:42,300 --> 00:01:42,940
rails.

49
00:01:44,040 --> 00:01:46,060
Trucks can move almost anywhere a road

50
00:01:46,060 --> 00:01:47,060
has been built.

51
00:01:47,520 --> 00:01:49,480
But during and after the Second World

52
00:01:49,480 --> 00:01:51,660
War, the United States military

53
00:01:51,660 --> 00:01:54,100
repeatedly encountered another kind of

54
00:01:54,100 --> 00:01:55,140
logistics problem.

55
00:01:55,660 --> 00:01:57,800
Moving heavy equipment through enormous

56
00:01:57,800 --> 00:01:59,880
areas where neither system

57
00:01:59,880 --> 00:02:00,840
existed.

58
00:02:01,500 --> 00:02:03,820
The Cold War made that problem

59
00:02:03,820 --> 00:02:05,240
even more urgent.

60
00:02:05,880 --> 00:02:07,960
Across Alaska, Northern Canada, and

61
00:02:07,960 --> 00:02:08,480
Greenland,

62
00:02:08,980 --> 00:02:11,400
the United States and Canada were building

63
00:02:11,400 --> 00:02:13,680
the distant early warning line,

64
00:02:14,260 --> 00:02:16,640
a chain of radar installations positioned

65
00:02:16,640 --> 00:02:17,840
across the Arctic.

66
00:02:18,540 --> 00:02:21,140
Their purpose was to provide early warning

67
00:02:21,140 --> 00:02:23,660
of a possible attack approaching across the

68
00:02:23,660 --> 00:02:24,560
polar region.

69
00:02:25,380 --> 00:02:27,240
But before a radar station could watch

70
00:02:27,240 --> 00:02:27,900
the Arctic,

71
00:02:28,320 --> 00:02:29,860
somebody had to build it.

72
00:02:30,020 --> 00:02:31,780
That meant transporting construction

73
00:02:31,780 --> 00:02:32,580
machinery,

74
00:02:33,020 --> 00:02:33,380
fuel,

75
00:02:33,980 --> 00:02:34,600
personnel,

76
00:02:35,340 --> 00:02:36,420
electrical equipment,

77
00:02:36,900 --> 00:02:37,760
and supplies.

78
00:02:38,400 --> 00:02:40,440
All into some of the least developed

79
00:02:40,650 --> 00:02:43,200
and least accessible terrain on the

80
00:02:43,200 --> 00:02:43,680
continent.

81
00:02:44,560 --> 00:02:46,720
There were places with no highways waiting

82
00:02:46,720 --> 00:02:47,700
for a convoy,

83
00:02:48,100 --> 00:02:50,540
no rail network running to the construction

84
00:02:50,540 --> 00:02:51,200
site.

85
00:02:51,980 --> 00:02:54,580
Sometimes there was barely a route at

86
00:02:54,580 --> 00:02:55,160
all.

87
00:02:55,880 --> 00:02:57,620
The army needed a vehicle that could

88
00:02:57,620 --> 00:02:59,700
carry serious loads across wilderness

89
00:02:59,700 --> 00:03:02,260
without first requiring somebody to build

90
00:03:02,260 --> 00:03:04,600
an entire transportation network for

91
00:03:04,600 --> 00:03:06,480
it. In other words,

92
00:03:07,040 --> 00:03:09,700
it wanted a transportation system that

93
00:03:09,700 --> 00:03:11,900
did not depend on another transportation

94
00:03:11,900 --> 00:03:14,220
system already being there.

95
00:03:14,600 --> 00:03:16,980
And one American engineer had already

96
00:03:16,980 --> 00:03:19,580
spent years experimenting with exactly

97
00:03:19,580 --> 00:03:20,640
that idea.

98
00:03:20,900 --> 00:03:23,000
Robert Gilmour -Lauterno made his name

99
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:24,720
building heavy machinery.

100
00:03:25,400 --> 00:03:27,020
But one of the ideas that made

101
00:03:27,020 --> 00:03:29,460
his machines unusual was not simply

102
00:03:29,460 --> 00:03:32,060
bigger engines or bigger tires.

103
00:03:32,680 --> 00:03:33,940
It was electricity.

104
00:03:34,740 --> 00:03:37,460
A conventional truck uses an engine,

105
00:03:37,700 --> 00:03:38,360
a transmission,

106
00:03:38,880 --> 00:03:41,520
drive shafts, and axles to send

107
00:03:41,520 --> 00:03:43,340
mechanical power to its wheels.

108
00:03:44,140 --> 00:03:46,400
That arrangement works well when the vehicle

109
00:03:46,400 --> 00:03:48,380
is one compact machine.

110
00:03:49,080 --> 00:03:51,140
It becomes much more complicated when the

111
00:03:51,140 --> 00:03:53,040
vehicle starts growing into a chain of

112
00:03:53,040 --> 00:03:54,120
powered sections,

113
00:03:54,700 --> 00:03:55,860
hundreds of feet long.

114
00:03:56,640 --> 00:03:58,140
The turn -o approached the problem

115
00:03:58,140 --> 00:03:58,780
differently.

116
00:03:59,200 --> 00:04:01,140
An engine could turn a generator.

117
00:04:01,700 --> 00:04:03,920
The generator could produce electricity.

118
00:04:04,480 --> 00:04:06,780
And that electricity could be sent to

119
00:04:06,780 --> 00:04:08,540
traction motors at the wheels.

120
00:04:09,220 --> 00:04:11,280
Now the source of power and the

121
00:04:11,280 --> 00:04:13,980
wheels using that power did not have

122
00:04:13,980 --> 00:04:16,240
to be connected by one enormous

123
00:04:16,240 --> 00:04:18,080
mechanical drive train.

124
00:04:19,260 --> 00:04:21,180
cables could carry the energy through the

125
00:04:21,180 --> 00:04:21,700
machine

126
00:04:22,100 --> 00:04:24,460
and that changed what was possible.

127
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:26,500
A trailer no longer had to be

128
00:04:26,500 --> 00:04:28,480
nothing more than dead weight behind one

129
00:04:28,480 --> 00:04:29,000
tractor.

130
00:04:30,080 --> 00:04:32,620
sections could contribute traction of their

131
00:04:32,620 --> 00:04:33,140
own.

132
00:04:33,900 --> 00:04:35,520
Another section could be added,

133
00:04:35,940 --> 00:04:36,740
then another.

134
00:04:37,280 --> 00:04:39,120
And power could be distributed through the

135
00:04:39,120 --> 00:04:39,620
train

136
00:04:40,080 --> 00:04:41,860
instead of asking one tractor at the

137
00:04:41,860 --> 00:04:44,540
front to drag everything behind it.

138
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:46,680
The turn -o had been developing electric

139
00:04:46,680 --> 00:04:48,720
wheel technology long before the

140
00:04:48,720 --> 00:04:49,820
TC497.

141
00:04:51,200 --> 00:04:53,320
Patents filed in the early 1950s

142
00:04:53,320 --> 00:04:55,860
described vehicle wheels incorporating

143
00:04:55,860 --> 00:04:58,000
electric traction motors directly

144
00:04:58,000 --> 00:04:59,840
into the wheel assembly.

145
00:05:00,380 --> 00:05:02,260
The basic idea was simple enough to

146
00:05:02,260 --> 00:05:03,740
explain in a diagram.

147
00:05:04,400 --> 00:05:06,700
Its consequences were enormous.

148
00:05:06,700 --> 00:05:09,400
The TC497 was

149
00:05:09,400 --> 00:05:11,280
not the first land train.

150
00:05:12,100 --> 00:05:13,800
It was the result of years of

151
00:05:13,800 --> 00:05:15,920
increasingly ambitious experiments.

152
00:05:16,740 --> 00:05:18,540
The turn -o had already developed the

153
00:05:18,540 --> 00:05:21,100
turn -o -train concept for carrying heavy

154
00:05:21,100 --> 00:05:23,640
loads through remote areas where

155
00:05:23,640 --> 00:05:25,820
normal transportation infrastructure was

156
00:05:25,820 --> 00:05:26,400
limited.

157
00:05:27,040 --> 00:05:29,260
Then came machines designed specifically for

158
00:05:29,260 --> 00:05:30,460
snow and tundra.

159
00:05:30,960 --> 00:05:33,260
One of them was the Snow Buggy,

160
00:05:33,520 --> 00:05:36,060
an experimental vehicle riding on enormous

161
00:05:36,060 --> 00:05:37,460
low -pressure tyres.

162
00:05:38,240 --> 00:05:40,100
turn -o was also involved with the

163
00:05:40,100 --> 00:05:42,340
VC22 Snow Freighter,

164
00:05:42,840 --> 00:05:45,460
a long articulated machine built

165
00:05:45,460 --> 00:05:47,360
for hauling cargo through the north.

166
00:05:48,120 --> 00:05:49,700
The idea was strange,

167
00:05:50,240 --> 00:05:52,340
but it was not purely theoretical.

168
00:05:53,520 --> 00:05:55,180
Machines based on this concept actually

169
00:05:55,180 --> 00:05:57,160
operated in arctic conditions.

170
00:05:58,020 --> 00:05:59,580
The army then asked the turn -o

171
00:05:59,580 --> 00:06:01,500
to combine the logic of the land

172
00:06:01,500 --> 00:06:04,080
train with the enormous tyres and cross

173
00:06:04,080 --> 00:06:06,560
-country mobility being developed for snow

174
00:06:06,560 --> 00:06:07,140
vehicles.

175
00:06:08,140 --> 00:06:10,500
The result was the Logistics Cargo

176
00:06:10,500 --> 00:06:11,320
Carrier,

177
00:06:12,100 --> 00:06:13,760
the LCC -1.

178
00:06:14,600 --> 00:06:16,620
It could haul about 45 tonnes,

179
00:06:17,440 --> 00:06:19,080
and it was successful enough that the

180
00:06:19,080 --> 00:06:21,140
army did not walk away from the

181
00:06:21,140 --> 00:06:21,680
concept.

182
00:06:22,160 --> 00:06:24,940
Instead, it asked for something bigger,

183
00:06:25,920 --> 00:06:26,660
much bigger.

184
00:06:27,360 --> 00:06:29,520
By the end of the 1950s, the

185
00:06:29,520 --> 00:06:31,140
question was no longer whether a multi

186
00:06:31,140 --> 00:06:33,700
-section, electrically -driven, off -road

187
00:06:33,700 --> 00:06:34,980
train could exist.

188
00:06:35,680 --> 00:06:37,980
The question was how far the

189
00:06:37,980 --> 00:06:39,400
concept could be pushed.

190
00:06:39,540 --> 00:06:42,240
The answer became the TC497

191
00:06:42,240 --> 00:06:44,960
Overland Train Mark II.

192
00:06:45,540 --> 00:06:47,960
From the front, the control car already

193
00:06:47,960 --> 00:06:49,100
looked unusual.

194
00:06:49,560 --> 00:06:52,020
It rose high above its giant tyres,

195
00:06:52,800 --> 00:06:54,800
more like a small building on wheels

196
00:06:55,190 --> 00:06:56,640
than the cab of a truck.

197
00:06:57,600 --> 00:07:00,260
Behind it came cargo sections and additional

198
00:07:00,260 --> 00:07:01,280
power units.

199
00:07:01,920 --> 00:07:04,380
In the full army configuration described at

200
00:07:04,380 --> 00:07:04,820
Yuma,

201
00:07:05,220 --> 00:07:07,220
the entire system contained

202
00:07:07,220 --> 00:07:08,760
13 units

203
00:07:09,170 --> 00:07:11,660
and stretched 572

204
00:07:11,660 --> 00:07:13,960
feet from end to end.

205
00:07:14,600 --> 00:07:17,220
The machine rode on tyres roughly 10

206
00:07:17,220 --> 00:07:18,220
feet tall.

207
00:07:18,980 --> 00:07:20,580
54 wheels were driven.

208
00:07:21,260 --> 00:07:23,880
A six -person crew operated the system,

209
00:07:24,100 --> 00:07:25,940
and the army said the train could

210
00:07:25,940 --> 00:07:28,600
carry about 150 tonnes of

211
00:07:28,600 --> 00:07:29,180
cargo.

212
00:07:30,260 --> 00:07:31,080
vehicles,

213
00:07:31,820 --> 00:07:32,740
construction equipment,

214
00:07:33,220 --> 00:07:33,840
containers.

215
00:07:34,820 --> 00:07:37,300
The final design used gas turbine engines

216
00:07:37,300 --> 00:07:39,900
to generate electrical power for the traction

217
00:07:39,900 --> 00:07:40,600
system.

218
00:07:41,160 --> 00:07:43,420
Instead of one enormous engine

219
00:07:43,420 --> 00:07:45,340
mechanically pulling a line of passive

220
00:07:45,340 --> 00:07:45,980
trailers,

221
00:07:46,400 --> 00:07:48,660
the Overland Train could distribute

222
00:07:48,660 --> 00:07:50,400
power through the machine.

223
00:07:51,040 --> 00:07:53,460
The result looked excessive because

224
00:07:53,460 --> 00:07:55,780
almost every normal assumption about a truck

225
00:07:55,780 --> 00:07:57,160
had been removed.

226
00:07:57,880 --> 00:07:59,420
The front vehicle did not have to

227
00:07:59,420 --> 00:08:01,980
provide all of the traction by itself.

228
00:08:02,580 --> 00:08:04,880
The sections behind it did not have

229
00:08:04,880 --> 00:08:06,720
to remain completely passive,

230
00:08:07,100 --> 00:08:09,340
and the overall length did not have

231
00:08:09,340 --> 00:08:11,760
to stop where a conventional mechanical drive

232
00:08:11,760 --> 00:08:13,840
train became impractical.

233
00:08:14,740 --> 00:08:16,320
The LeTourneau had effectively turned

234
00:08:16,320 --> 00:08:18,920
something resembling a convoy into

235
00:08:18,920 --> 00:08:20,480
one connected machine.

236
00:08:21,580 --> 00:08:24,260
Army historical accounts place its maximum

237
00:08:24,260 --> 00:08:26,760
speed at roughly 20 miles per

238
00:08:26,760 --> 00:08:27,260
hour,

239
00:08:27,860 --> 00:08:30,460
and its operating range at around 400

240
00:08:30,460 --> 00:08:31,240
miles.

241
00:08:32,040 --> 00:08:34,040
Those figures would be unimpressive for an

242
00:08:34,040 --> 00:08:35,420
ordinary highway truck.

243
00:08:35,980 --> 00:08:38,600
But the TC497 was not

244
00:08:38,600 --> 00:08:40,260
built for an ordinary highway.

245
00:08:41,160 --> 00:08:43,680
Its entire purpose was to go where

246
00:08:43,680 --> 00:08:45,340
the highway did not exist.

247
00:08:45,500 --> 00:08:48,000
But building a machine this long created

248
00:08:48,000 --> 00:08:49,920
a problem that had nothing to do

249
00:08:49,920 --> 00:08:50,720
with horsepower.

250
00:08:51,580 --> 00:08:52,880
How do you make it turn?

251
00:08:53,700 --> 00:08:56,200
A normal truck pulling a trailer does

252
00:08:56,200 --> 00:08:58,740
not trace one perfect path around

253
00:08:58,740 --> 00:08:59,380
a corner.

254
00:08:59,960 --> 00:09:02,680
The trailer cuts inside the tractor's turn.

255
00:09:03,480 --> 00:09:04,580
Add another trailer,

256
00:09:05,060 --> 00:09:07,460
and the geometry becomes more difficult.

257
00:09:08,440 --> 00:09:10,820
Now imagine that problem repeated through a

258
00:09:10,820 --> 00:09:12,740
vehicle hundreds of feet long.

259
00:09:13,600 --> 00:09:15,820
If every section simply behaved like an

260
00:09:15,820 --> 00:09:17,200
ordinary road trailer,

261
00:09:17,600 --> 00:09:19,280
the rear of the train could cut

262
00:09:19,280 --> 00:09:21,640
dramatically across the inside of a turn.

263
00:09:22,040 --> 00:09:24,320
On a road, that might mean leaving

264
00:09:24,320 --> 00:09:25,100
the pavement.

265
00:09:26,000 --> 00:09:28,620
In rough terrain, it could mean encountering

266
00:09:28,620 --> 00:09:29,500
obstacles,

267
00:09:29,980 --> 00:09:32,520
or ground the control car had avoided.

268
00:09:33,600 --> 00:09:36,200
So the TC497 could

269
00:09:36,200 --> 00:09:38,380
not behave exactly like a conventional

270
00:09:38,380 --> 00:09:40,180
tractor -trailer combination.

271
00:09:41,020 --> 00:09:43,340
Its steering system was designed so the

272
00:09:43,340 --> 00:09:45,580
sections of the train could more closely

273
00:09:45,580 --> 00:09:47,860
follow the path established by the lead

274
00:09:47,860 --> 00:09:48,420
vehicle.

275
00:09:49,120 --> 00:09:50,980
The machine also had to deal with

276
00:09:50,980 --> 00:09:52,320
another basic problem,

277
00:09:52,900 --> 00:09:53,400
traction.

278
00:09:54,640 --> 00:09:56,420
If one tractor attempted to drag a

279
00:09:56,420 --> 00:09:58,900
massive load through loose sand using

280
00:09:58,900 --> 00:10:01,460
only a few driven wheels, those wheels

281
00:10:01,460 --> 00:10:02,460
could lose grip,

282
00:10:02,860 --> 00:10:05,300
while the trailers behind it became

283
00:10:05,300 --> 00:10:06,040
anchors.

284
00:10:07,200 --> 00:10:09,340
Distributed electric drive changed that

285
00:10:09,340 --> 00:10:10,080
relationship.

286
00:10:11,140 --> 00:10:13,140
Power could be delivered to wheels throughout

287
00:10:13,140 --> 00:10:15,360
the system, and the enormous

288
00:10:15,360 --> 00:10:17,660
tyres spread the vehicle's load

289
00:10:17,660 --> 00:10:20,120
over a larger contact area than

290
00:10:20,120 --> 00:10:21,700
conventional truck tyres.

291
00:10:22,440 --> 00:10:24,860
The result was a machine designed around

292
00:10:24,860 --> 00:10:27,380
cross -country mobility, rather than

293
00:10:27,380 --> 00:10:28,500
highway performance.

294
00:10:29,400 --> 00:10:31,240
But the Army wanted to know exactly

295
00:10:31,240 --> 00:10:33,320
how effective that approach really

296
00:10:33,320 --> 00:10:34,120
was.

297
00:10:34,560 --> 00:10:36,580
So it did not simply photograph the

298
00:10:36,580 --> 00:10:38,740
TC497 in the desert

299
00:10:38,930 --> 00:10:41,220
and declare the experiment successful.

300
00:10:42,460 --> 00:10:43,580
studied the terrain.

301
00:10:44,220 --> 00:10:45,400
They measured the ground.

302
00:10:45,920 --> 00:10:48,440
They planned specific test routes.

303
00:10:49,220 --> 00:10:50,740
And they tried to connect what happened

304
00:10:50,740 --> 00:10:52,680
to the machine with measurable

305
00:10:52,680 --> 00:10:55,400
characteristics of the landscape beneath it.

306
00:10:55,880 --> 00:10:57,900
That effort became Project

307
00:10:57,900 --> 00:10:58,720
OTTER.

308
00:10:59,320 --> 00:11:01,960
Overland train terrain evaluation

309
00:11:01,960 --> 00:11:04,040
research. And while the conventional

310
00:11:04,040 --> 00:11:05,260
TC497

311
00:11:05,880 --> 00:11:08,300
was already an extraordinary machine,

312
00:11:09,020 --> 00:11:11,240
the Army briefly considered taking the

313
00:11:11,240 --> 00:11:12,780
concept much further.

314
00:11:13,600 --> 00:11:15,120
In 1961,

315
00:11:15,580 --> 00:11:18,160
an Army research publication discussed

316
00:11:18,160 --> 00:11:20,740
using a compact nuclear power plant

317
00:11:20,740 --> 00:11:23,180
as a possible source of energy for

318
00:11:23,180 --> 00:11:24,460
an overland train.

319
00:11:25,660 --> 00:11:27,800
The idea followed naturally from the

320
00:11:27,800 --> 00:11:29,780
vehicle's electrical architecture.

321
00:11:30,740 --> 00:11:33,300
If the wheels were driven electrically, then

322
00:11:33,300 --> 00:11:35,120
the traction system did not fundamentally

323
00:11:35,120 --> 00:11:37,320
require a conventional mechanical

324
00:11:37,320 --> 00:11:39,860
connection between the power plant and the

325
00:11:39,860 --> 00:11:40,440
wheels.

326
00:11:41,320 --> 00:11:43,620
Electricity generated by another source

327
00:11:43,620 --> 00:11:45,400
could, at least in principle,

328
00:11:45,880 --> 00:11:48,140
feed the same kind of distributed drive

329
00:11:48,140 --> 00:11:48,780
system.

330
00:11:49,500 --> 00:11:51,620
A compact reactor was therefore considered

331
00:11:51,620 --> 00:11:54,260
as one possible way to give a

332
00:11:54,260 --> 00:11:56,740
future cross -country vehicle extremely

333
00:11:56,740 --> 00:11:58,000
long endurance,

334
00:11:58,420 --> 00:12:01,020
far from conventional fuel infrastructure.

335
00:12:02,100 --> 00:12:04,700
But the nuclear version never became the

336
00:12:04,700 --> 00:12:05,740
TC497

337
00:12:06,320 --> 00:12:08,000
that drove across Yuma.

338
00:12:08,600 --> 00:12:10,900
The actual machine used

339
00:12:10,900 --> 00:12:12,140
gas turbines.

340
00:12:13,180 --> 00:12:15,260
Still, the proposal shows how seriously the

341
00:12:15,260 --> 00:12:18,000
Army was thinking about the broader concept.

342
00:12:19,040 --> 00:12:21,320
For a brief period, the overland train

343
00:12:21,320 --> 00:12:23,460
was not being treated merely as an

344
00:12:23,460 --> 00:12:24,780
oversized truck.

345
00:12:25,600 --> 00:12:27,720
It was being considered as something closer

346
00:12:27,720 --> 00:12:30,160
to a mobile logistics system.

347
00:12:30,280 --> 00:12:32,720
The TC497 arrived at

348
00:12:32,720 --> 00:12:34,820
Yuma in 1962,

349
00:12:35,300 --> 00:12:37,660
where Army testing began in the

350
00:12:37,660 --> 00:12:38,780
Arizona desert.

351
00:12:39,580 --> 00:12:42,040
Yuma offered exactly the kind of environment

352
00:12:42,040 --> 00:12:44,380
the Army needed for a machine like

353
00:12:44,380 --> 00:12:45,300
this.

354
00:12:46,900 --> 00:12:48,260
uneven surfaces,

355
00:12:49,020 --> 00:12:49,380
slopes,

356
00:12:50,160 --> 00:12:51,020
loose ground,

357
00:12:51,500 --> 00:12:54,080
and enormous open areas where engineers

358
00:12:54,080 --> 00:12:56,740
could observe how an unconventional vehicle

359
00:12:56,740 --> 00:12:58,560
behaved away from roads.

360
00:12:59,320 --> 00:13:01,440
The formal project OTTER terrain

361
00:13:01,440 --> 00:13:03,820
evaluation work documented by the

362
00:13:03,820 --> 00:13:06,380
Army Corps of Engineers followed in February

363
00:13:06,380 --> 00:13:08,880
and June of 1963.

364
00:13:10,340 --> 00:13:12,380
Project OTTER was not simply a durability

365
00:13:12,380 --> 00:13:13,300
test.

366
00:13:14,340 --> 00:13:16,420
were trying to connect measurable terrain

367
00:13:16,420 --> 00:13:19,000
characteristics at Yuma with the actual

368
00:13:19,000 --> 00:13:20,540
performance of the train.

369
00:13:21,120 --> 00:13:22,860
They also wanted to understand whether

370
00:13:22,860 --> 00:13:24,960
similar terrain conditions could be

371
00:13:24,960 --> 00:13:27,660
identified in other desert regions around the

372
00:13:27,660 --> 00:13:28,220
world.

373
00:13:28,780 --> 00:13:30,480
That distinction matters.

374
00:13:31,060 --> 00:13:33,220
A vehicle that can cross one carefully

375
00:13:33,220 --> 00:13:35,320
selected demonstration route is

376
00:13:35,320 --> 00:13:36,080
interesting.

377
00:13:36,780 --> 00:13:38,800
A vehicle whose performance can be related

378
00:13:38,800 --> 00:13:41,080
to measurable terrain is

379
00:13:41,080 --> 00:13:42,780
potentially much more useful.

380
00:13:42,920 --> 00:13:44,720
The test program did not become as

381
00:13:44,720 --> 00:13:47,420
extensive as planners had originally hoped.

382
00:13:48,500 --> 00:13:50,640
According to the final Project OTTER report,

383
00:13:50,960 --> 00:13:53,360
testing ended after only a limited number

384
00:13:53,360 --> 00:13:55,300
of courses had been traversed.

385
00:13:55,720 --> 00:13:58,180
That left engineers with less quantitative

386
00:13:58,180 --> 00:13:59,980
data than originally planned.

387
00:14:00,760 --> 00:14:03,240
But what they did observe is revealing.

388
00:14:04,000 --> 00:14:06,460
The report states that the Overland train

389
00:14:06,460 --> 00:14:07,960
crossed course 12

390
00:14:08,480 --> 00:14:10,780
and a shortened version of course one

391
00:14:11,120 --> 00:14:13,720
without becoming immobilized because of the

392
00:14:13,720 --> 00:14:14,340
terrain.

393
00:14:15,060 --> 00:14:17,560
That was significant for a machine more

394
00:14:17,560 --> 00:14:19,240
than 500 feet long.

395
00:14:19,880 --> 00:14:22,520
But the report also recorded a clear

396
00:14:22,520 --> 00:14:23,680
physical limit.

397
00:14:24,180 --> 00:14:26,760
In the sand hills, the TC497

398
00:14:26,760 --> 00:14:29,420
could not negotiate a 12 foot

399
00:14:29,420 --> 00:14:31,040
high dune slip face.

400
00:14:31,880 --> 00:14:33,780
That is exactly the kind of result

401
00:14:33,780 --> 00:14:36,280
a serious engineering test is supposed

402
00:14:36,280 --> 00:14:37,100
to produce.

403
00:14:37,780 --> 00:14:40,300
Not proof that a machine is unstoppable

404
00:14:40,900 --> 00:14:43,120
and not proof that it is useless.

405
00:14:44,000 --> 00:14:44,820
A boundary.

406
00:14:45,700 --> 00:14:47,900
The Overland train demonstrated

407
00:14:47,900 --> 00:14:49,940
real cross -country mobility.

408
00:14:50,560 --> 00:14:53,160
But difficult terrain was not

409
00:14:53,160 --> 00:14:54,060
all the same.

410
00:14:54,740 --> 00:14:56,000
And this is where the story of

411
00:14:56,000 --> 00:14:57,060
the TC497

412
00:14:57,760 --> 00:15:00,260
becomes more interesting than the usual

413
00:15:00,260 --> 00:15:02,800
story of an oversized military

414
00:15:02,800 --> 00:15:03,660
experiment.

415
00:15:04,380 --> 00:15:06,760
Because the Army's own later account

416
00:15:06,760 --> 00:15:09,320
does not describe the vehicle as a

417
00:15:09,320 --> 00:15:11,400
machine that simply failed its tests.

418
00:15:11,940 --> 00:15:14,280
It says the Overland train performed

419
00:15:14,280 --> 00:15:15,660
well during testing.

420
00:15:15,660 --> 00:15:17,900
Project OTTER gives that

421
00:15:17,900 --> 00:15:19,980
statement useful context.

422
00:15:20,800 --> 00:15:22,980
The machine demonstrated genuine cross

423
00:15:22,980 --> 00:15:24,220
-country mobility.

424
00:15:24,840 --> 00:15:27,540
It negotiated the tested courses without

425
00:15:27,540 --> 00:15:29,820
terrain induced immobilization on those

426
00:15:29,820 --> 00:15:30,360
routes.

427
00:15:30,840 --> 00:15:32,880
And it did so while carrying the

428
00:15:32,880 --> 00:15:35,000
complexity of a vehicle hundreds

429
00:15:35,000 --> 00:15:36,400
of feet long.

430
00:15:37,240 --> 00:15:39,400
At the same time, Project OTTER

431
00:15:39,400 --> 00:15:41,420
documented very real limits.

432
00:15:42,020 --> 00:15:44,160
The train could not ignore geography.

433
00:15:44,160 --> 00:15:46,840
Certain terrain features could stop

434
00:15:46,840 --> 00:15:47,420
it.

435
00:15:47,900 --> 00:15:50,300
And the test program itself was shorter

436
00:15:50,300 --> 00:15:51,780
than originally intended.

437
00:15:52,700 --> 00:15:54,540
So saying the TC497

438
00:15:54,540 --> 00:15:56,880
worked does not mean

439
00:15:56,880 --> 00:15:58,180
saying it was perfect.

440
00:15:58,880 --> 00:16:00,720
It means something more specific.

441
00:16:01,380 --> 00:16:03,480
The fundamental concept had been

442
00:16:03,480 --> 00:16:04,240
demonstrated.

443
00:16:04,980 --> 00:16:07,560
A train -sized logistic system could

444
00:16:07,560 --> 00:16:10,180
move across difficult country without

445
00:16:10,180 --> 00:16:12,360
rails and without depending

446
00:16:12,360 --> 00:16:14,680
on a conventional road network.

447
00:16:15,460 --> 00:16:17,200
Which creates the obvious question.

448
00:16:17,980 --> 00:16:20,000
If the concept actually worked,

449
00:16:20,820 --> 00:16:23,200
why did the Army never build a

450
00:16:23,200 --> 00:16:23,980
fleet of them?

451
00:16:24,640 --> 00:16:27,140
The answer was already appearing above

452
00:16:27,140 --> 00:16:28,220
the test range.

453
00:16:28,920 --> 00:16:31,120
The same military logistics problem

454
00:16:31,120 --> 00:16:33,500
that had produced the Overland train

455
00:16:33,740 --> 00:16:36,180
was being attacked from an entirely

456
00:16:36,180 --> 00:16:37,460
different direction.

457
00:16:38,280 --> 00:16:40,140
Heavy -lift helicopters were becoming

458
00:16:40,140 --> 00:16:41,960
increasingly practical.

459
00:16:42,220 --> 00:16:44,220
A land train addressed the absence of

460
00:16:44,220 --> 00:16:46,320
roads by building a vehicle capable of

461
00:16:46,320 --> 00:16:48,660
crossing difficult ground without them.

462
00:16:49,460 --> 00:16:51,520
lift attacked the same problem

463
00:16:51,520 --> 00:16:54,120
by reducing how much the ground mattered

464
00:16:54,120 --> 00:16:55,400
in the first place.

465
00:16:56,200 --> 00:16:57,820
That difference was profound.

466
00:16:58,600 --> 00:17:00,700
A ground vehicle still had to negotiate

467
00:17:00,700 --> 00:17:02,320
the terrain in front of it.

468
00:17:02,880 --> 00:17:03,400
mattered.

469
00:17:04,220 --> 00:17:05,260
Steep ridges mattered.

470
00:17:05,260 --> 00:17:07,320
Soft ground mattered.

471
00:17:08,020 --> 00:17:09,480
Large dunes mattered.

472
00:17:10,240 --> 00:17:12,400
A heavy -lift helicopter could bypass many

473
00:17:12,400 --> 00:17:15,140
of those terrain obstacles entirely.

474
00:17:16,120 --> 00:17:18,420
Aircrafts such as Sikorsky's S -64

475
00:17:18,420 --> 00:17:20,600
Sky Crane and the Army's

476
00:17:20,600 --> 00:17:22,680
CH -54 Tarhe

477
00:17:22,680 --> 00:17:25,020
represented a fundamentally different

478
00:17:25,020 --> 00:17:27,540
solution to the logistics problem.

479
00:17:28,080 --> 00:17:30,460
Instead of carrying a supply system slowly

480
00:17:30,460 --> 00:17:32,120
across difficult terrain,

481
00:17:32,520 --> 00:17:35,020
vertical lift could move heavy loads

482
00:17:35,020 --> 00:17:37,320
directly between points without

483
00:17:37,320 --> 00:17:39,740
following the landscape between them.

484
00:17:40,400 --> 00:17:42,920
historical accounts are unusually clear about

485
00:17:42,920 --> 00:17:44,120
what happened next.

486
00:17:44,700 --> 00:17:47,140
Heavy -lift helicopter technology made the

487
00:17:47,140 --> 00:17:49,400
Overland train increasingly obsolete

488
00:17:49,400 --> 00:17:51,480
before the concept could become an

489
00:17:51,480 --> 00:17:52,580
operational fleet.

490
00:17:53,440 --> 00:17:55,640
The Army later described vertical lift

491
00:17:55,640 --> 00:17:58,160
as a faster, more flexible and

492
00:17:58,160 --> 00:18:00,840
more economical solution for the mission it

493
00:18:00,840 --> 00:18:02,220
had been trying to solve.

494
00:18:02,960 --> 00:18:05,480
The TC -497 had spent

495
00:18:05,480 --> 00:18:07,960
years becoming better at dealing with the

496
00:18:07,960 --> 00:18:08,400
ground.

497
00:18:09,260 --> 00:18:11,660
Then another technology changed how

498
00:18:11,660 --> 00:18:13,920
necessary dealing with the ground was.

499
00:18:14,380 --> 00:18:16,940
The Overland train was not defeated because

500
00:18:16,940 --> 00:18:18,980
it immediately broke down in the desert.

501
00:18:19,460 --> 00:18:21,220
It was not defeated because the basic

502
00:18:21,220 --> 00:18:22,700
concept could not move.

503
00:18:23,360 --> 00:18:25,860
It was defeated by a competing technology

504
00:18:25,860 --> 00:18:28,340
that approached the same problem from an

505
00:18:28,340 --> 00:18:29,980
entirely different direction.

506
00:18:30,900 --> 00:18:32,560
It wasn't defeated by the desert,

507
00:18:33,020 --> 00:18:35,160
it was defeated by the sky.

508
00:18:35,500 --> 00:18:38,080
The TC -497 never became

509
00:18:38,080 --> 00:18:40,080
an operational Army fleet.

510
00:18:40,680 --> 00:18:41,880
In 1968,

511
00:18:42,380 --> 00:18:44,740
the Overland train was sold for scrap.

512
00:18:45,320 --> 00:18:47,500
The enormous chain of cargo and power

513
00:18:47,500 --> 00:18:48,960
sections disappeared.

514
00:18:49,560 --> 00:18:52,240
But one part survived, the control

515
00:18:52,240 --> 00:18:52,820
car.

516
00:18:53,320 --> 00:18:55,460
For a time, it stood in Yuma

517
00:18:55,460 --> 00:18:57,920
as an advertising display for an RV

518
00:18:57,920 --> 00:18:58,620
dealership.

519
00:18:59,620 --> 00:19:02,040
Decades later, it returned to Yuma

520
00:19:02,040 --> 00:19:02,960
proving ground.

521
00:19:03,780 --> 00:19:04,780
In 2019,

522
00:19:05,260 --> 00:19:07,600
volunteers cleaned and repainted its

523
00:19:07,600 --> 00:19:08,220
interior.

524
00:19:08,940 --> 00:19:11,300
And the surviving control car remains there

525
00:19:11,300 --> 00:19:12,980
as a static display.

526
00:19:13,760 --> 00:19:15,360
Without the rest of the train behind

527
00:19:15,360 --> 00:19:17,920
it, the cab can look almost incomplete.

528
00:19:18,560 --> 00:19:21,180
A strange tower on giant tyres.

529
00:19:21,740 --> 00:19:23,520
With nowhere left to go.

530
00:19:24,220 --> 00:19:26,240
But it is also the perfect surviving

531
00:19:26,240 --> 00:19:27,520
piece of the project.

532
00:19:28,160 --> 00:19:30,720
The front of a transportation system built

533
00:19:30,720 --> 00:19:33,320
for a future that never quite arrived.

534
00:19:33,520 --> 00:19:35,920
For years, engineers had been trying to

535
00:19:35,920 --> 00:19:37,560
answer a difficult question.

536
00:19:38,280 --> 00:19:40,100
How do you move an enormous supply

537
00:19:40,100 --> 00:19:42,320
line through a place where roads and

538
00:19:42,320 --> 00:19:43,960
railroads do not exist?

539
00:19:44,900 --> 00:19:47,460
The TC -497 was one

540
00:19:47,460 --> 00:19:48,840
serious answer.

541
00:19:49,340 --> 00:19:50,140
Not a joke.

542
00:19:50,480 --> 00:19:52,820
Not simply an engineering failure.

543
00:19:53,520 --> 00:19:55,420
And not just a machine made enormous

544
00:19:55,420 --> 00:19:57,220
for the sake of being enormous.

545
00:19:58,160 --> 00:20:00,460
For a brief period, it represented a

546
00:20:00,460 --> 00:20:03,100
plausible vision of how military logistics

547
00:20:03,100 --> 00:20:05,320
might work across some of the most

548
00:20:05,320 --> 00:20:07,560
inaccessible terrain on Earth.

549
00:20:08,460 --> 00:20:10,120
Then another answer arrived.

550
00:20:10,840 --> 00:20:13,000
Almost all of the Overland train was

551
00:20:13,000 --> 00:20:13,780
cut apart.

552
00:20:14,320 --> 00:20:16,260
But the control car remained.

553
00:20:16,740 --> 00:20:19,120
A monument to an era when

554
00:20:19,120 --> 00:20:20,940
America's answer to the end of the

555
00:20:20,940 --> 00:20:23,540
road was to build something that

556
00:20:23,540 --> 00:20:25,060
no longer needed one.

